A||||6||Viper'sVenom||Working with Fiberglass||||1||
Z||000000||Viper'sVenom||09-24-2001||02:30 PM||||I want to customize my interior and for the the dash and console I have decided to use fiberglass.  Can anyone tell me what tools I need to use for this stuff.  I have gotten as far as finding out that I need the fiberglass mat and resin.  Can anyone recommend brands and where you might find it.  Also can you use that spray foam for a mold to lay the fiberglass down on.  I once read in a kit car magazine that you needed all these specialized tools, but that was a long time ago I would assume that new products have probably come out that don't need them.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||09-24-2001||03:27 PM||||I've redone mine with lots of resin, trunk liner carpet(no fiberglass mat), bondo, bondo-glass, plywood.<P>Its a pain to do, but worth it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Plan on many many hours of work.<P>Oh...buy a big ol' box of throw away brushes.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">   <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-24-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000002||Sage||09-24-2001||08:58 PM||||You can use clay-wood-plaster-bondo-some types of foam or just about anything that resin won't stick to, to build a "plug" or "buck" or form that you want your finished piece to look like.  If you are not going to make a mold, then it has to be the thickness of the cloth and resin you are going to add, smaller than you want the finished piece to be.  You can use just about any kind of foam if after getting it shaped, you cover it with masking tape or tin foil or any material that will act as a barrier between your plug and the cloth and resin you are going to add.  Some foams,(urethanes) are impervious to the resin, but the styrene content in the resin will eat most commonly found foam.  After you decide on how and from what material to make your plug, you cover it with at least 3 layers of cloth and resin.  Cut the cloth so that it will take the shape of your plug, then wet it out off the plug, and then drape it onto the plug and using a natural bristle brush,(the resin will eat any other type), work it onto your plug leaving some excess on the overhanging edges.  Then trim it off with a sharp knife after it "kicks" to a semi solid state,(about 30 min, after you layed it up IF you mixed the resin right), but before it gets completely "hard".  If you wait too long, you will have use a grinder to trim the edges.  You want to be able to pull the part off the plug when you are done.  Put no more than 3 layers on at a time.  Put each layer on seperately and work in, but do them in fast succession with the same batch of resin you mixed up.  Follow the mixing ratio exactly.  Most of the over the counter stuff is a 2% mix of hardner to resin.  Most beginners have an easier time using matte rather than cloth.  Also most of the resin you can get over the counter is what is known as "call" resin.  It is from the first batch mixed up when they are calibrating the machines, so it will be a little bit different each time you buy a new supply.  The equipment has gotten pretty sophisticted, so even the call resin has gotten better than it used to be.  After you get your piece built up where you want it,(3 or 4 layers SHOULD be thick enough if you made your plug right),then you will probably end up sanding off about a layer.  Lots of finishing work doing it this way, unless you are going to cover it in fabric or something. In which case the finish doesn't really matter, only the shape.  If you want to end up with a piece you can paint, then you have to sand and fill, sand and fill till you get what you want.  You can use bondo or polyester glazing putty for filler.<P>This is a very scaled down basic description of one way to do what you were asking about.  The better way is to make a mold, but I'm not even going to attempt explaining that here.  Yea, you can build a "makeshift" mold that will throw one or maybe two parts, but a good quality, long lasting mold is a little more involved!  If you want to investigate this subject further and see what products and information is available look up fiberglast.com in Dayton Oh.  They are a large fiberglass supply house.<P>Hope this helps you a little.<P>Have a good one!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Viper'sVenom||09-24-2001||09:21 PM||||Sage I really appreciate the help.  And what did you mean by sanding then filling?  Why do you need to fill it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sage||09-24-2001||09:34 PM||||The glass will be rough on the side opposite where it is in contact with the plug.  If you want a smooth finish,(suitable for painting), you will have to finish it by sanding down high spots and filling low spots, just like you would a fender or hood or any other body panel, if you were repairing it after a wreck, prepping it for paint.  If you really want some good info on working with glass, look up the site I mentioned.  They offer free off the net printable tech guides in a number of fiberglass areas.<P>Have fun!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sootah||09-25-2001||12:41 AM||||If you do make a dash, photodocument the hell out of it. We'll all wanna see how it goes.<P>Also, has anybody ever tried to fibreglass over another dash? Meaning buy a decent dash from a boneyard and using it as the mold? Building up the areas you want and all that. I've got a cool idea for a dash, but not sure I can do it. Somebody wanna make something and then write up a tut on how to do it?<P>Thanx now,<BR>-<B><I>Sootah</I></B><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">  <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
